---
import PublishedLayout from '../../components/layout/PublishedLayout.astro';
---

<PublishedLayout pageIntentId="pi-feature-reference-conventions">
  <section class="page-heading wrapper">
    <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
    <h1>Measurement and reference conventions</h1>
    <p>
      This page explains how to read Fitwise dimensions and where internal reference values differ
      from product measurements or published standards.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="wrapper section">
    <h2>Measurement types</h2>
    <dl class="conventions">
      <div>
        <dt>Exact</dt>
        <dd>A value transcribed from the cited source or exact model documentation.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Nominal</dt>
        <dd>
          A marketed or conventional size label. It may not equal the item's outside dimensions.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Typical</dt>
        <dd>A representative example, not a guarantee for every model or configuration.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Recommended</dt>
        <dd>
          A sourced recommendation or an explicitly labeled Fitwise planning target. Check the
          accompanying note to distinguish them.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Derived</dt>
        <dd>A calculation from named source dimensions or a stated geometric formula.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <h2>Internal reference values</h2>
    <p>
      Some examples use round-number reference dimensions or comfort targets when no single external
      standard applies. These are Fitwise modeling conventions, not product specifications, laws,
      building-code values or universal ergonomic minimums. When the UI asks for a user-selected
      clearance, the result uses that input and does not claim it is a standard.
    </p>
    <h2>Units, precision and rounding</h2>
    <p>
      Geometry is calculated internally in millimetres. Inputs may use mm, cm, m, inches or feet;
      visible dimensions pass through the shared measurement formatter. Rounding is for display only
      and does not change intermediate geometry. Screen-panel dimensions derived from diagonal and
      aspect ratio are estimates rounded to the nearest millimetre and are not overall device
      widths.
    </p>
    <h2>How to interpret results</h2>
    <p>
      “Physical margin” is available space minus the hard footprint. “Target margin” is available
      space minus the selected or sourced recommended envelope. A positive physical margin does not
      mean a clearance target is met; compare both columns. Planning permission, installation
      requirements, structural suitability and safety are not evaluated unless a tool explicitly
      says otherwise—and no generic local regulation is inferred.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/methodology/">Return to calculation methodology</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</PublishedLayout>

<style>
  .page-heading {
    padding-block: var(--space-7) var(--space-3);
  }

  .page-heading h1 {
    margin-block: var(--space-1) var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--fs-3xl);
    letter-spacing: -0.035em;
  }

  .page-heading > p:last-child,
  .section > p {
    max-width: 78ch;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-accent-1);
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .section {
    padding-block: var(--space-6);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .conventions {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    max-width: 78ch;
  }

  .conventions div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(6rem, 0.3fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-3);
    padding-block: var(--space-2);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .conventions dt {
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .conventions dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
</style>
